WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.120
Picture a theater in your mind.

00:02.220 --> 00:02.360
Okay.

00:02.580 --> 00:03.680
You've got the stage, right?

00:04.220 --> 00:09.080
Solid wooden floorboards, heavy curtains, fixed securely in place.

00:09.100 --> 00:10.700
Like a classic passive background.

00:10.800 --> 00:11.180
Exactly.

00:11.720 --> 00:16.500
And then you have the actors moving around that stage, delivering their lines, executing the choreography of the play.

00:17.240 --> 00:23.740
And, well, if someone were to ask you to explain how a computer works, or honestly, even how your own brain works,

00:24.260 --> 00:29.100
you would very likely use a similar image, even if you didn't, you know, realize it.

00:29.100 --> 00:33.200
Right. There's the hardware, the fixed stage, and the software, the actors.

00:33.280 --> 00:36.920
Yeah, the electrical impulses, the thoughts moving across it.

00:36.920 --> 00:39.620
It feels like a very clean, comforting separation.

00:39.920 --> 00:44.060
Like the actors do all the acting, and the stage simply holds them up.

00:44.180 --> 00:47.520
It is the ultimate classical view of reality.

00:47.760 --> 00:52.640
I mean, that underlying assumption that the environment where the action happens, the space of possible states,

00:52.680 --> 00:54.880
is just a permanent passive background.

00:54.960 --> 00:55.680
You see this everywhere.

00:55.680 --> 01:01.000
From, you know, high school physics classes to the actual way we design microchips.

01:01.440 --> 01:04.240
But imagine if that mental model was completely wrong.

01:04.600 --> 01:12.800
Like, what if, every single time an actor took a step, the heel of their boot literally crushed and reshaped the wooden floorboards beneath them?

01:12.860 --> 01:14.620
That would be a very expensive production.

01:14.920 --> 01:15.180
Right.

01:15.180 --> 01:19.060
But imagine the stage is actually made of, I don't know, wet, heavy clay.

01:20.020 --> 01:26.760
By the middle of the second act, the stage is so warped, so deeply rutted and deformed by the actor's own footsteps,

01:27.180 --> 01:30.240
that certain scenes simply cannot be performed anymore.

01:30.380 --> 01:32.440
Because the physical geometry won't allow it.

01:32.500 --> 01:32.900
Exactly.

01:33.360 --> 01:37.740
The actors physically cannot reach stage left because there's literally a sinkhole there now.

01:37.740 --> 01:39.820
The stage isn't just holding the actors.

01:40.240 --> 01:45.520
The stage is actively dictating what the actors are mathematically capable of doing next.

01:45.880 --> 01:50.540
And to complete the loop, the actors are the ones who made the stage that way through their previous actions.

01:51.060 --> 01:56.180
It's this continuous, inescapable cycle of cause and effect between the process and the medium itself.

01:56.640 --> 02:03.580
So today, we are taking a deep dive into an independent research paper from June 2026 by an author named Flyxion.

02:04.080 --> 02:06.960
It's titled, Computation on Evolving Substrates.

02:06.960 --> 02:09.480
It's a dense one, but incredibly rewarding.

02:09.960 --> 02:10.620
Oh, absolutely.

02:10.960 --> 02:20.480
And the mission for today is to give you, listening to this right now, a fundamental aha moment about the hidden physics of how things change.

02:21.360 --> 02:25.040
We are going to completely shatter the illusion of the fixed stage.

02:25.240 --> 02:25.940
We really are.

02:26.000 --> 02:32.400
We're looking at everything from the fluid sloshing around inside your skull to the literal words you are hearing me say right now

02:32.400 --> 02:38.320
to, honestly, why the software you use at work is so unbelievably frustrating.

02:38.840 --> 02:46.100
Though, to even approach the math in this paper, we actually have to unlearn a cognitive error that almost all of us make every single day.

02:46.200 --> 02:46.680
Which is?

02:46.800 --> 02:50.060
The author formally refers to it as the noun fallacy.

02:50.060 --> 02:56.160
The noun fell as, okay, let's unpack this because I read this part and immediately felt a little, well, defensive.

02:56.340 --> 02:57.740
It challenges how we speak, yeah.

02:58.100 --> 02:58.800
We love nouns.

02:59.520 --> 03:01.200
I look at a river and I call it a river.

03:01.500 --> 03:02.200
It is a thing.

03:02.340 --> 03:03.540
I can pray to it on a map.

03:03.660 --> 03:04.860
I can, you know, swim in it.

03:05.120 --> 03:08.100
Are we really starting off by arguing that a river isn't a thing?

03:08.240 --> 03:08.760
We are.

03:08.900 --> 03:10.760
But not just to be difficult, I promise.

03:10.980 --> 03:11.100
Yeah.

03:11.200 --> 03:13.220
Think about what a river actually is.

03:13.220 --> 03:17.660
Well, the water you were pointing at today is entirely different from the water that was there yesterday.

03:18.100 --> 03:19.480
And the banks have eroded slightly.

03:19.680 --> 03:20.700
The sediment has shifted.

03:21.280 --> 03:28.540
The river is really just our lazy, compressed, human way of labeling an ongoing, relentless process.

03:28.700 --> 03:29.160
Oh, I see.

03:29.260 --> 03:30.520
Flowing is what is real.

03:30.900 --> 03:32.320
The noun, the river, is just an illusion.

03:33.060 --> 03:39.340
We treat dynamic, ongoing processes as stable objects because our brains just need to save energy.

03:39.640 --> 03:39.860
Okay.

03:39.860 --> 03:44.420
I suppose that makes sense for a river, but how does that become a scientific fallacy?

03:45.060 --> 03:49.820
Because this cognitive habit bleeds directly into our most rigorous scientific models.

03:50.360 --> 03:54.100
It's baked into economics, software engineering, biology.

03:54.860 --> 04:00.460
These fields routinely rely on what the paper calls a frozen manifold approximation.

04:00.820 --> 04:01.500
Stop right there.

04:01.600 --> 04:02.800
Frozen manifold approximation.

04:03.060 --> 04:04.180
You're going to have to translate that for me.

04:04.420 --> 04:09.160
I know what a frozen manifold is on a rusty car engine, but I'm guessing that's not what we're talking about here.

04:09.160 --> 04:09.620
Fair enough.

04:09.700 --> 04:10.380
No cars here.

04:10.820 --> 04:16.680
In mathematics and physics, a manifold is simply the geometric space of all possible states a system can inhabit.

04:17.120 --> 04:19.260
Think of it as the admissibility geometry.

04:20.040 --> 04:25.740
It defines the rules of the game, what moves are currently allowed, what futures are reachable from the present moment.

04:26.260 --> 04:34.080
The frozen part means that our models assume this geometry, this rulebook, does not change while the system is running.

04:34.080 --> 04:36.720
So it's like treating the rules of the world like a rigid chessboard.

04:37.120 --> 04:38.060
The squares are fixed.

04:38.260 --> 04:42.160
The rules of how a knight moves are absolute, regardless of what turn it is.

04:42.220 --> 04:42.660
Exactly.

04:43.060 --> 04:50.240
But the author of this paper argues that the frozen manifold is, at best, a highly fragile first order approximation.

04:50.880 --> 04:51.120
Right.

04:51.340 --> 05:01.240
It completely falls apart the moment a system's substrate, the physical or conceptual medium through which the actions actually travel, is altered by the actions themselves.

05:01.240 --> 05:06.080
Meaning the chessboard isn't wood, it's that wet clay from our theater analogy earlier.

05:06.160 --> 05:07.020
Yes, precisely.

05:07.380 --> 05:12.660
Every single time I move my knight, the physical weight of the piece makes the squares stretch and sink.

05:13.040 --> 05:15.480
The actual geometry of the board deforms.

05:16.120 --> 05:22.400
So by turn 10, the physical distance between my bishop and my opponent's king might be completely torn apart.

05:22.400 --> 05:30.400
Right. A move that was totally valid on turn 1 is now mathematically impossible, entirely because of the moves you made to get to turn 10.

05:30.560 --> 05:31.400
That's wild.

05:31.620 --> 05:36.040
And this isn't just philosophy, you know. It's a severe problem in applied mathematics.

05:36.620 --> 05:38.720
Take industrial control theory, for instance.

05:38.920 --> 05:39.720
Okay, Leo Oni.

05:39.720 --> 05:45.260
Engineers need to control complex systems, like a chemical plant or a robotic arm.

05:45.880 --> 05:54.520
To make the math solvable, they separate the system they are controlling, they call it the plant, from the constraints, which are the limits of what the system can safely do.

05:54.620 --> 05:58.340
Makes sense. Plant over here, constraints over there, nice and clean.

05:58.340 --> 06:05.920
But what happens when the exhaust or the intense vibration from the plant actually degrades the metal constraints in real time?

06:06.280 --> 06:08.880
That clean mathematical wall just collapses.

06:09.500 --> 06:11.340
The stage has become one of the actors.

06:11.700 --> 06:11.760
Wow.

06:11.940 --> 06:14.860
The constraint geometry isn't a background rulebook anymore.

06:15.280 --> 06:20.060
It is a living, breathing history of every single trajectory the system has ever taken.

06:20.460 --> 06:24.080
I want to ground this, because otherwise we're just, you know, floating in abstract math.

06:24.460 --> 06:27.080
And the paper doesn't just theorize about this.

06:27.080 --> 06:30.740
It points to a physical reality inside the listener's own head.

06:31.060 --> 06:35.440
The biological blueprint for this whole theory is the human brain.

06:36.220 --> 06:44.260
Specifically, what the author calls the electrophluidic brain, which is a pretty radical departure from mainstream neuroscience.

06:44.640 --> 06:44.840
Right.

06:44.980 --> 06:50.280
Because if I open a biology textbook right now, the undisputed star of the show is the neuron.

06:50.440 --> 06:51.680
The famous neuron, yes.

06:51.740 --> 06:52.700
The electrical spikes.

06:52.900 --> 06:53.080
Yeah.

06:53.160 --> 06:54.900
The digital zeros and ones of the brain.

06:54.900 --> 06:56.920
And that's what we hook up to EG machines.

06:57.100 --> 06:57.860
That's what we map.

06:57.980 --> 06:59.400
That's what we completely obsess over.

06:59.460 --> 07:00.860
Because it's easy to measure, right?

07:00.900 --> 07:01.040
Yeah.

07:01.260 --> 07:04.000
And it looks like a computer circuit, which is a comforting metaphor.

07:04.700 --> 07:10.920
But this paper builds on recent reviews in neuroscience, specifically worked by Chakravarty and colleagues,

07:10.920 --> 07:16.840
to argue that we've been ignoring a slower, older, analog layer of computation.

07:16.840 --> 07:17.660
Which is what?

07:17.880 --> 07:22.740
The extracellular space, the cerebrospinal fluid, the vascular networks, and the glial cells.

07:23.020 --> 07:24.520
The stuff between the neurons.

07:25.200 --> 07:26.080
The fluid.

07:26.400 --> 07:35.540
I mean, I was always taught that glial cells and brain fluid were basically just life support, like the passive scaffolding that keeps the important stuff to neurons alive.

07:35.540 --> 07:38.720
That view is becoming rapidly obsolete, thankfully.

07:39.600 --> 07:41.740
This fluid space isn't passive packaging.

07:42.140 --> 07:49.160
It is a dynamic substrate that actively constrains and is continuously reshaped by neural activity.

07:49.380 --> 07:49.600
Okay.

07:49.960 --> 07:57.640
To model this, the paper introduces a dimensional framework called RSVP, relativistic scalar vector plenum.

07:58.060 --> 07:59.880
I'm going to need you to ELI-5 that.

07:59.980 --> 08:01.000
Explain it like I'm 5.

08:01.460 --> 08:03.340
RSVP sounds like a wedding invitation.

08:03.340 --> 08:05.640
What do those three fields actually mean physically?

08:05.860 --> 08:06.880
We can definitely break it down.

08:07.120 --> 08:08.700
It's a model of three coupled fields.

08:08.960 --> 08:09.060
Right.

08:09.240 --> 08:12.320
First is capacity, denoted by the Greek letter phi.

08:12.720 --> 08:13.140
Phi, okay.

08:13.220 --> 08:15.160
This is the local energetic potential.

08:15.460 --> 08:23.860
In your physical brain, this translates to the literal availability of calcium or potassium ions in a specific microscopic pocket of extracellular fluid.

08:24.280 --> 08:28.320
If the capacity is high, nearby neurons have the resources to fire a spike.

08:28.420 --> 08:29.160
If it's low.

08:29.260 --> 08:31.100
If it's depleted, they physically cannot.

08:31.100 --> 08:31.660
Got it.

08:31.660 --> 08:33.400
Capacity is the fuel in the area.

08:33.840 --> 08:37.020
Second, we have transport, represented by a vector V.

08:37.480 --> 08:39.660
This is the directed flow of that fluid.

08:40.180 --> 08:42.740
The cerebrospinal fluid is not just sitting still.

08:43.080 --> 08:51.480
It is constantly sloshing, driven by your heartbeat and respiration, moving those vital ions around, channeling influence from one brain region to another.

08:51.660 --> 08:51.880
Okay.

08:52.220 --> 08:55.480
So fuel is phi, flow is V, and the third field.

08:55.480 --> 09:01.640
Dissipation or S. This is the irreversible metabolic burden, the wear and tear.

09:01.640 --> 09:04.020
What do you mean by wear and tear in the brain?

09:04.420 --> 09:07.460
Well, when your neurons fire, they don't just magically reset.

09:07.780 --> 09:12.640
They leave behind metabolic waste, beta amyloid proteins, tau proteins, oxidative byproducts.

09:13.360 --> 09:16.920
That waste physically clogs up the extracellular space.

09:17.060 --> 09:18.740
Let me try an analogy here to see if I've got it.

09:18.960 --> 09:20.260
Think of a city's traffic grid.

09:20.320 --> 09:20.520
Okay.

09:20.660 --> 09:21.480
I like where this is going.

09:21.480 --> 09:26.340
The capacity, the phi, that is the open, empty asphalt and the gasoline in the cars.

09:26.960 --> 09:31.500
The transport, the V, is the actual dynamic slow of the cars moving down the avenues.

09:31.920 --> 09:39.300
And the dissipation, the S, that's the smog building up in the air, the engine heat, and the literal potholes forming on the streets because of the tires.

09:39.680 --> 09:40.960
A very apt comparison.

09:41.900 --> 09:47.000
Now, imagine you are a city planner trying to predict where traffic will jam up tomorrow.

09:47.000 --> 09:53.020
Mainstream neuroscience has basically been trying to predict traffic by only looking at the cars.

09:53.860 --> 09:55.040
But you can't just look at the cars.

09:55.280 --> 09:57.540
You have to study the potholes and the road closures.

09:58.100 --> 10:03.860
Because the cars create the potholes today, and those potholes dictate where the cars can physically drive tomorrow.

10:04.240 --> 10:05.760
It is a closed feedback loop.

10:05.940 --> 10:10.700
And this brings us to the mathematical core of the paper, the dynamic substrate theorem.

10:11.340 --> 10:16.340
The author provides a dimensional analysis of these fields, and it reveals something crucial.

10:16.340 --> 10:16.900
Which is?

10:17.460 --> 10:23.400
Dissipation and capacity share the exact same dimensional units resource per unit volume.

10:23.640 --> 10:24.580
Why is that a big deal?

10:24.700 --> 10:27.620
Because it proves they are physically two sides of the same coin.

10:28.100 --> 10:30.960
Dissipation isn't some abstract tiredness factor.

10:31.400 --> 10:37.320
It is literally consumed capacity that occupies the exact same physical volume but hasn't been cleared away yet.

10:37.320 --> 10:42.700
So, when a fast neural spike happens, it isn't just sending an electrical signal into the void.

10:43.140 --> 10:48.500
It is physically eating local capacity and converting it into local dissipation.

10:48.700 --> 10:50.540
It alters the ionic gradients.

10:50.680 --> 10:52.580
It changes the thickness of the fluid.

10:53.080 --> 10:58.200
The thought you just had physically reshaped the geometric space of your brain.

10:58.380 --> 10:59.320
That is wild.

10:59.620 --> 11:01.020
The brain is not a circuit board.

11:01.140 --> 11:01.460
Yeah.

11:01.460 --> 11:06.040
A silicon circuit board doesn't change its physical shape when electricity runs through a trace.

11:06.160 --> 11:06.680
Exactly.

11:07.020 --> 11:09.300
The brain is a co-evolving landscape.

11:10.040 --> 11:14.280
Fast events, electrical spikes operate over slowly evolving fluid substrates.

11:14.380 --> 11:16.120
But wait, I have to push back here for a second.

11:16.300 --> 11:16.620
Go ahead.

11:16.620 --> 11:22.180
If the fluid and the waste products are doing so much heavy lifting, dictating what neurons can and cannot do,

11:22.720 --> 11:26.400
how do we explain things like pacemakers or deep brain stimulation?

11:27.060 --> 11:30.760
We use purely electrical implants to cure tremors in Parkinson's patients.

11:31.260 --> 11:36.200
If the analog fluid is the boss, why does hacking the electrical layer work so well?

11:36.420 --> 11:37.740
That is an excellent challenge.

11:38.080 --> 11:40.740
The paper doesn't argue the electrical layer is powerless.

11:40.980 --> 11:43.020
It argues it's part of a coupled system.

11:43.020 --> 11:49.020
When you use deep brain stimulation, you are forcing a massive external electrical override.

11:50.280 --> 11:54.840
You are essentially blasting the traffic grid with a megaphone telling all cars to stop.

11:55.120 --> 12:00.460
It works, but it's a brute force intervention that often comes with severe side effects over time

12:00.460 --> 12:03.860
because it completely ignores the underlying fluid substrate.

12:04.160 --> 12:04.680
I see.

12:04.700 --> 12:09.120
You are forcing the neurons to fire regardless of the local capacity or dissipation,

12:09.240 --> 12:11.280
which can actually lead to tissue degradation.

12:11.280 --> 12:17.080
Ah, so we are forcing the actors to dance even if the floorboards are cracking underneath them.

12:17.320 --> 12:17.800
Precisely.

12:18.260 --> 12:21.180
Which leads to a really interesting historical mystery.

12:21.640 --> 12:25.820
If this electrophluidic reality, this dynamic wet clay,

12:25.940 --> 12:29.240
has been happening inside our skulls for the entire history of our species,

12:30.380 --> 12:34.540
why did it take neuroscience over a century to even start looking at it this way?

12:34.640 --> 12:34.900
Right.

12:35.020 --> 12:39.840
If this is the hidden physics of the mind, how did thousands of brilliant scientists just miss it?

12:39.840 --> 12:45.100
Because of how humans measure complex systems, we rely on simplified projections.

12:45.640 --> 12:50.840
And the paper argues this introduces a catastrophic error called admissibility distortion,

12:51.200 --> 12:56.820
which is explored through a framework called CLIO, the Constraint Layered Inference Ontology.

12:57.260 --> 12:58.320
Admissibility distortion.

12:58.320 --> 13:01.340
That sounds incredibly severe.

13:01.340 --> 13:04.780
I imagine trying to describe a complex argument I had with a friend,

13:05.120 --> 13:10.260
and by summarizing it, I leave out the tone of voice, which completely distorts who was actually mad.

13:10.600 --> 13:12.820
That's a perfect behavioral example.

13:13.320 --> 13:17.980
You projected a high-dimensional event, the argument, down to a low-dimensional summary, the word spoken.

13:17.980 --> 13:22.380
By doing so, you threw away the causal variables, the tone, that explain what happens next.

13:22.980 --> 13:26.240
In science, the neuron doctrine has dominated since the days of Kajal.

13:26.680 --> 13:33.080
It looks at the staggering, overwhelming complexity of the biological brain and projects it down to just spikes and synapses.

13:33.400 --> 13:37.400
It looks at the electricity and throws away the fluid, the glia, the vascular pressure.

13:37.740 --> 13:40.400
But I feel like I need to defend the scientists here for a minute.

13:40.880 --> 13:42.140
Don't we have to simplify?

13:42.140 --> 13:47.060
We cannot track every single molecule of calcium in a human brain.

13:47.460 --> 13:48.700
We don't have the computing power.

13:49.160 --> 13:54.420
If a meteorologist wants to predict a hurricane, they can't track every butterfly flapping its wings.

13:55.120 --> 13:57.800
Simplification is the only reason science works.

13:58.240 --> 13:59.540
You are absolutely correct.

13:59.700 --> 14:01.480
Simplification is non-negotiable.

14:01.900 --> 14:04.460
But the type of simplification is what matters.

14:04.700 --> 14:07.100
Theorem 3.3 in the text states that

14:07.100 --> 14:12.320
Discarding substrate variables that alter propagation incurs admissibility distortion.

14:12.480 --> 14:12.760
You mean?

14:12.880 --> 14:17.620
Meaning if you throw away the variables that physically determine what the system is allowed to do next,

14:17.780 --> 14:20.560
your model loses the ability to predict the future.

14:20.860 --> 14:21.200
Period.

14:21.420 --> 14:22.380
So it's a blind spot.

14:22.540 --> 14:23.440
A huge one.

14:23.760 --> 14:26.200
Under this distortion, you could put a patient in a scanner

14:26.200 --> 14:30.260
and observe two brain states that look identical to our neuron-tracking instruments.

14:30.740 --> 14:32.000
The spikes are exactly the same.

14:32.080 --> 14:33.460
The synapses match perfectly.

14:33.620 --> 14:34.680
But the fluid is different.

14:34.980 --> 14:35.380
Exactly.

14:35.380 --> 14:40.600
Underneath the electrical projection, one state has depleted calcium and high metabolic waste,

14:40.780 --> 14:43.080
and the other is fresh and fully resourced.

14:43.480 --> 14:45.240
Because their substrates are different,

14:45.560 --> 14:49.180
those two identical-looking states have completely different reachable futures.

14:49.920 --> 14:54.380
One might be able to form a new memory, and the other might fail to fire entirely.

14:54.800 --> 14:57.900
The classical model is totally blind to this divergence.

14:58.320 --> 15:02.880
It just sees the same input producing two different outputs and calls it biological noise.

15:02.880 --> 15:05.660
That is terrifying for medical research.

15:05.760 --> 15:05.980
No.

15:06.140 --> 15:12.000
But what absolutely blew my mind reading this paper is that it doesn't just keep this argument in the realm of biology.

15:12.340 --> 15:19.620
It proves this is a measurable physical law by pointing to a laboratory confirmation in quantum many-body physics.

15:19.900 --> 15:20.200
Yes.

15:20.380 --> 15:23.780
The Zeng et al. experiment on quantum computational advantage.

15:23.780 --> 15:25.000
It is a stunning parallel.

15:25.000 --> 15:28.740
Okay, but you cannot just drop terms like quantum many-body physics and keep going.

15:28.880 --> 15:29.000
Oh.

15:29.060 --> 15:30.360
I need you to translate this entirely.

15:30.700 --> 15:34.900
How does quantum mechanics prove that ignoring brain fluid ruins your data?

15:35.260 --> 15:36.260
Let's walk through it carefully.

15:37.180 --> 15:42.760
When physicists try to study a complex quantum system using a classical computer, they run into a wall.

15:42.760 --> 15:49.020
The number of possible states in a quantum system grows exponentially with every particle you add.

15:49.040 --> 15:49.900
It really gets too big.

15:50.140 --> 16:00.460
A classical computer simply doesn't have enough memory to track it all, so physicists use a mathematical compression technique called a matrix product state, or MPS.

16:00.640 --> 16:06.880
A compression technique, like taking a high-res photo and saving it as a low-res JPEG so it fits on a hard drive.

16:06.880 --> 16:17.480
Exactly. MPS compresses the quantum state by throwing away complex, long-range correlations between particles and keeping only the local, most obvious interactions.

16:18.320 --> 16:24.700
Now, when the quantum system is calm, what physicists call a localized phase, this JPEG compression works beautifully.

16:25.200 --> 16:27.880
The degrees of freedom it threw away weren't doing much anyway.

16:28.040 --> 16:30.640
It's a sunny day. The low-res photo still looks like a sunny day.

16:30.640 --> 16:38.900
But then the system enters a highly active thermalized state, specifically something called a floquet phase, where the system is being driven by a periodic pulse of energy.

16:39.440 --> 16:43.460
When this happens, a phenomenon called volume law entanglement takes over.

16:43.680 --> 16:46.560
I'm stopping you again. What is volume law entanglement?

16:47.260 --> 16:54.720
Entanglement is when particles become mathematically linked, so what happens to one instantly affects the other, no matter how far apart they are.

16:54.720 --> 17:00.260
Volume law means that this entanglement isn't just happening between next-door neighbors anymore.

17:00.660 --> 17:03.620
It is spreading across the entire volume of the system.

17:04.120 --> 17:08.080
Every particle is deeply, causally linked to every other particle.

17:08.140 --> 17:10.740
A massive, tangled web of cause and effect.

17:10.920 --> 17:15.720
Exactly. And this is where the classical computer's MPS compression fails catastrophically.

17:15.720 --> 17:20.940
The compression algorithm is designed to throw away those complex, long-range links.

17:21.240 --> 17:26.240
So the classical computer looks at the system, strips away the entanglement, and says, okay, here is the state.

17:26.500 --> 17:30.640
It might even look at two different quantum states and declare them mathematically identical.

17:31.040 --> 17:32.040
Because it threw away the differences.

17:32.380 --> 17:32.900
Precisely.

17:33.120 --> 17:33.240
Yeah.

17:33.320 --> 17:40.860
But in reality, their reachable futures, specifically their 14th-order density correlations, diverge wildly in the next fraction of a second.

17:40.860 --> 17:45.320
The classical projection incurred total admissibility distortion.

17:45.860 --> 17:50.160
It lost the future because it threw away the causal substrate of the system, the entanglement.

17:50.560 --> 17:52.720
Wow. So it's the exact same error.

17:53.220 --> 18:01.300
The classical computer throwing away quantum entanglement is mathematically identical to a neuroscientist throwing away the cerebrospinal fluid.

18:01.980 --> 18:08.360
If your measurement discards the physical medium that limits what can happen next, your math no longer matches the territory.

18:08.800 --> 18:09.900
You are flying blind.

18:09.900 --> 18:12.080
Which brings us to the obvious crisis.

18:12.660 --> 18:19.520
If our measurements and our standard scientific models are constantly distorting reality by projecting away the substrate, how do we fix it?

18:19.640 --> 18:21.340
We can't just stop doing science.

18:21.440 --> 18:22.540
We need a repair manual.

18:23.260 --> 18:28.640
And the paper gives us two incredibly different real-world examples of how this distortion gets fixed in practice.

18:29.240 --> 18:32.260
Let's look at the first mechanism, substrate remodeling.

18:32.580 --> 18:38.100
This is demonstrated by an empirical study by Cohen et al. on Deep Neural Network's artificial intelligence.

18:38.100 --> 18:39.600
I found this fascinating.

18:40.320 --> 18:44.280
Think about an untrained AI, just a raw neural network.

18:44.740 --> 18:47.260
You wanted to learn how to recognize a picture of a cat.

18:47.680 --> 18:53.940
If you feed it a raw image of a cat and then another image of the exact same cat but in the slightly darker room,

18:54.580 --> 18:59.080
those two raw pixel patterns occupy completely different places in the AI's brain.

18:59.080 --> 19:06.300
To the untrained AI, they are a tangled, chaotic mess, utterly impossible to separate from a picture of a dog.

19:06.500 --> 19:06.760
Right.

19:07.260 --> 19:13.480
The standard projection treating the raw pixel data as the only relevant reality incurs severe admissibility distortion.

19:13.920 --> 19:17.020
Two pixel patterns that look completely different might both be cats.

19:17.020 --> 19:19.780
So what does the AI actually do during training?

19:19.920 --> 19:25.220
I always thought training was just, you know, handing the AI a giant cheat sheet of correct answers until it memorized them.

19:25.340 --> 19:25.860
Not at all.

19:26.120 --> 19:31.540
The training process literally mathematically remodels the physical substrate of the AI's weight space.

19:31.820 --> 19:36.820
The paper points to three specific geometric measurements from the Cohen study to prove this.

19:36.960 --> 19:38.560
Okay, hit me with the math, but keep it grounded.

19:39.160 --> 19:45.740
The researchers tracked the manifold's effective radius, its effective dimension, and the intermanifold center correlations.

19:45.740 --> 19:48.340
I need an analogy for a manifold in an AI.

19:48.580 --> 19:53.720
Imagine you take a piece of paper and draw blue dots on it for cats and red dots for dogs.

19:53.840 --> 19:54.040
Okay.

19:54.220 --> 19:56.300
Now crumple that paper up into a tight ball.

19:56.880 --> 20:01.280
In that 3D crumpled ball, a blue dot might be physically touching a red dot.

20:01.580 --> 20:02.600
They are completely entangled.

20:03.040 --> 20:04.960
The manifold is the surface of that paper.

20:05.160 --> 20:06.060
Okay, I see it.

20:06.200 --> 20:11.240
During training, what the AI is actually doing is mathematically uncrumpling the paper.

20:11.240 --> 20:17.640
The effective dimension dropping means the tangled 3D ball is flattening out into a smooth 2D sheet.

20:17.960 --> 20:25.280
The effective radius shrinking means all the blue dots are being pulled closer together into a tight cluster far away from the red dots.

20:25.440 --> 20:28.980
Meaning the AI is actively smoothing out the clay of its own chessboard.

20:29.340 --> 20:36.180
It is reshaping its internal constraint geometry so that drawing a clean line between the cats and the dogs becomes mathematically reachable.

20:36.880 --> 20:38.300
Learning is not memorization.

20:38.740 --> 20:41.060
Learning is literal substrate repair.

20:41.240 --> 20:47.900
The AI fixes its own admissibility distortion by permanently deforming its internal geometry.

20:48.380 --> 20:52.080
It is a profound realization about what knowledge actually is geometrically.

20:52.600 --> 20:54.580
Now the second repair mechanism is completely different.

20:54.720 --> 20:59.220
It's called trajectory preserving projection and it relies on a study by Bush et al.

20:59.660 --> 21:02.220
Involving human fMRI brain scans.

21:02.620 --> 21:05.240
So the first fix was an AI changing its own brain.

21:05.240 --> 21:12.360
The second fix is about us, the scientists, realizing our math is broken and redesigning how we look at data.

21:12.760 --> 21:12.940
Correct.

21:13.440 --> 21:19.080
In this fMRI study, people are placed in a scanner and asked to watch naturalistic movies, just normal video clips.

21:19.460 --> 21:22.500
The researchers are looking for cognitive event boundaries.

21:22.760 --> 21:23.220
Which are what?

21:23.220 --> 21:28.100
That is the exact millisecond your brain transitions from processing one scene to the next.

21:28.100 --> 21:35.460
But the paper points out that standard analysis tools, like PCA printable component analysis, completely ruin the data.

21:36.040 --> 21:36.400
How?

21:36.620 --> 21:41.760
Because tools like PCA are designed to look at a massive data set and find the vectors of highest variance.

21:41.760 --> 21:49.220
To do that, they treat every single snapshot of the brain scan as a completely disconnected, isolated dot in a scatter plot.

21:49.520 --> 21:50.860
They collapse the flow of time.

21:51.220 --> 21:56.640
They take a flowing river, freeze it, chop it up into a million separate ice cubes and throw them in a bucket.

21:57.080 --> 21:59.400
And then they wonder why they can't find the river current.

21:59.700 --> 22:01.560
That is exactly what PCA does.

22:01.720 --> 22:04.040
It destroys the autocorrelation of the data.

22:04.040 --> 22:13.540
It incurs massive admissibility distortion because the cognitive structure they are looking for a scene transition does not exist in a single millisecond.

22:13.800 --> 22:19.320
It only exists in the slow, coherent drift of the brain state over several seconds.

22:19.700 --> 22:24.420
So the researchers swapped out PCA for a new mathematical projection called T-FATE.

22:25.060 --> 22:26.760
Tell me how T-FATE actually works.

22:27.180 --> 22:29.780
T-FHE incorporates an autocorrelation kernel.

22:29.780 --> 22:33.700
In simple terms, it forces the mathematical projection to respect time.

22:34.040 --> 22:36.620
It bakes the temporal adjacency back into the data.

22:37.100 --> 22:42.880
It explicitly tells the math, hey, remember that moment B happened immediately after moment A, and they are physically linked.

22:43.240 --> 22:46.540
And when they applied this math that respects time, what happened?

22:46.640 --> 22:47.600
The results were striking.

22:48.260 --> 22:54.040
Suddenly, the cognitive event boundaries emerged from the noise, perfectly aligned with the human behavior of watching the movie.

22:54.120 --> 22:54.780
It was there all along.

22:54.800 --> 22:57.700
The structure was there in the brain the entire time.

22:57.700 --> 23:03.740
It was only invisible because the previous scientific tools were fundamentally throwing away the dimension of time.

23:03.740 --> 23:06.140
I love the juxtaposition of those two examples.

23:06.140 --> 23:12.460
In the AI, the machine physically alters its reality to make sense of the world.

23:13.120 --> 23:17.660
In the fMRI, the humans alter their mathematical lens so they stop ignoring time.

23:18.420 --> 23:19.860
Both solve the exact same problem.

23:20.360 --> 23:22.300
They stop the loss of causal information.

23:22.720 --> 23:24.360
They preserve the future.

23:24.360 --> 23:28.060
This is a perfect pivot to the most philosophically profound part of the paper.

23:28.280 --> 23:32.120
We've established that at the bottom level, everything is a flowing, changing substrate.

23:32.740 --> 23:33.540
Everything is process.

23:33.860 --> 23:38.920
Everything is coupled, fields of capacity and dissipation continuously evolving together.

23:39.000 --> 23:39.140
Right.

23:39.300 --> 23:44.020
And here is where I, as a normal human being, have a minor existential crisis.

23:44.380 --> 23:44.700
Oh.

23:44.700 --> 23:49.400
Because if everything is just flowing fields and changing manifolds, why does my world look so solid?

23:49.520 --> 23:51.540
Why do I look at my desk and see a coffee cup?

23:51.760 --> 23:53.400
Why do I see discrete objects?

23:53.620 --> 23:57.100
Why isn't everything just a blurry, terrifying soup of trajectories?

23:57.360 --> 24:03.680
The paper anticipates this exact crisis and introduces a formal framework called SpherePop to answer it.

24:04.960 --> 24:10.600
SpherePop posits that objects, the nouns we love so much, are not primitive entities at all.

24:11.360 --> 24:13.960
Objects are collapsed process histories.

24:13.960 --> 24:15.620
Collapsed process histories.

24:15.680 --> 24:15.860
Okay.

24:15.880 --> 24:18.420
I was trying to visualize this and I came up with an analogy.

24:18.600 --> 24:19.360
Tell me if this tracks.

24:19.400 --> 24:19.920
SpherePop.

24:20.220 --> 24:25.080
Imagine a pristine, flat, grassy field in the middle of a college campus.

24:25.700 --> 24:26.800
It is a blank substrate.

24:27.020 --> 24:28.860
A flat admissibility geometry.

24:29.100 --> 24:29.560
Exactly.

24:30.120 --> 24:34.040
Now, students start walking across it to get from the library to their dorms.

24:34.440 --> 24:38.060
The first few students take random winding paths, trajectories.

24:38.340 --> 24:42.980
But after a few weeks, people start to follow the faintest footprints of the people who walk before them

24:42.980 --> 24:47.220
because the grass is slightly flattened, making it marginally easier to walk on.

24:47.220 --> 24:51.280
The local capacity of that specific trajectory has slightly increased.

24:51.720 --> 24:51.820
Right.

24:52.220 --> 24:54.660
And over a whole semester, the grass dies.

24:54.900 --> 24:55.900
The dirt gets packed down.

24:56.220 --> 24:57.480
A highly defined trench forms.

24:57.480 --> 25:05.100
Eventually, the university facilities team comes along, sees the dirt trench, and pours concrete over it to make an official sidewalk.

25:05.100 --> 25:12.460
Now, if a new student arrives next year and looks at that sidewalk, the paved path looks like a solid, permanent object.

25:12.840 --> 25:13.240
A noun.

25:14.020 --> 25:15.020
But what is it really?

25:15.380 --> 25:21.980
That solid path is actually the collapsed, compressed history of 10,000 individual footsteps.

25:21.980 --> 25:26.120
That is an absolutely flawless visualization of the mechanism of sphere pop.

25:26.420 --> 25:34.260
When multiple distinct trajectories yield the same stable description over and over, that stable description, that object, is just an equivalence class.

25:34.380 --> 25:36.080
It is the solid residue of history.

25:36.580 --> 25:40.560
The paper formalizes this mathematically as the object-as-collapse theorem.

25:40.720 --> 25:42.080
So things are not things.

25:42.300 --> 25:45.000
Things are just the scars left behind by repeated actions.

25:45.380 --> 25:48.260
Does this mean a biological neuron isn't a fundamental building block?

25:48.260 --> 25:48.860
Exactly.

25:49.520 --> 25:52.300
Under this framework, a neuron is not a primary object.

25:52.600 --> 26:01.220
A neuron is the collapsed, highly stable residue of millions of electrophluidic interactions that eventually found a deep attractor state in the biological geometry.

26:01.600 --> 26:04.780
This involves a property the paper calls collapse-idempotence.

26:05.020 --> 26:05.280
Yes.

26:05.520 --> 26:12.140
Idempotence is a property in mathematics where applying an operation multiple times doesn't change the result after the first time.

26:12.580 --> 26:14.600
For example, multiplying a number by one.

26:14.740 --> 26:15.020
Right.

26:15.020 --> 26:23.180
Once a process history has fully collapsed into a stable object, repeatedly applying the collapse operation yields the exact same object.

26:23.640 --> 26:25.520
The path is already paved concrete.

26:25.820 --> 26:28.040
You can compress it mathematically all you want.

26:28.120 --> 26:29.480
It remains the paved path.

26:30.180 --> 26:34.780
This is why stable objects appear so undeniably robust and real to our senses.

26:35.380 --> 26:40.440
They represent extremely deep, inescapable attractors in the admissibility geometry.

26:40.780 --> 26:43.080
It fundamentally flips how we do science.

26:43.080 --> 26:50.200
When a researcher looks through an electron microscope and sees a discrete neural circuit, they are not looking at the fundamental truth of the brain.

26:50.680 --> 26:52.360
They are looking at the paved path.

26:52.700 --> 26:53.920
They are looking at the concrete.

26:54.660 --> 27:03.080
The paper's plea is that we have to stop obsessing over the collapsed objects and start studying the hidden generating histories that form them in the snow.

27:03.080 --> 27:09.860
And if we abstract this one level further, we realize this mathematics doesn't just apply to brain fluid or quantum states.

27:10.380 --> 27:14.200
This dynamic substrate architecture governs the macroscopic world.

27:14.660 --> 27:16.720
It is the physics of human society.

27:17.120 --> 27:19.280
This is where the theory gets intensely personal.

27:19.280 --> 27:27.680
The paper argues that this exact same dynamic governs the language we speak, the software we use, and the governments we live under.

27:28.160 --> 27:33.800
Let's start with language, because the paper proposes a radical inversion of how we think about communication.

27:34.060 --> 27:36.180
Normally, we view communication as transmission.

27:36.860 --> 27:42.580
We think the signal, the audio wave of my voice, or the black ink on a page is the primary vessel.

27:42.580 --> 27:45.660
We think the signal literally contains the meaning.

27:46.000 --> 27:52.100
Like the words are little Amazon delivery trucks carrying packages of meaning from my brain across the air and dropping them into your brain.

27:52.240 --> 27:53.560
Yes, the conduit metaphor.

27:54.140 --> 27:59.480
But the paper argues that descriptions of the geometry in our heads are primary and signals are merely rendered from them.

28:00.200 --> 28:02.400
Text is not a signal encoding content.

28:02.960 --> 28:09.200
Text is a sequence of distinction operators that steer a trajectory through a collectively maintained constraint geometry.

28:09.580 --> 28:10.900
Let's slow down and examine that.

28:10.900 --> 28:17.320
When I speak a sentence to you, the actual sound waves I am generating are incredibly low bandwidth.

28:17.900 --> 28:18.560
They are tiny.

28:19.100 --> 28:23.360
Yet the amount of meaning, emotion, and context you receive is massive.

28:24.160 --> 28:27.280
How does that tiny audio signal carry so much weight?

28:28.040 --> 28:30.740
The paper calls this the semantic compression ratio.

28:30.740 --> 28:38.640
And the reason that ratio can be so astonishingly high is because you and I share an immense invisible substrate of unsaid context.

28:38.640 --> 28:43.520
We share English phonology, syntax, cultural assumptions, historical references.

28:43.760 --> 28:45.100
It completely explains jargon.

28:45.540 --> 28:48.240
Think about two master chefs in a kitchen during a dinner rush.

28:48.660 --> 28:50.660
One chef says two words, fire table.

28:51.300 --> 28:52.820
To an outsider, there's barely English.

28:52.980 --> 28:54.340
It contains almost no information.

28:54.600 --> 28:54.860
Right.

28:54.980 --> 29:01.100
But to the other chef, it means a highly specific cascade of 20 different actions involving heat, timing, and plating.

29:01.100 --> 29:03.080
The words aren't carrying the information.

29:03.680 --> 29:11.620
The words are just a tiny steering wheel guiding the listener through a massive pre-built admissibility manifold they both spent a decade building in culinary school.

29:11.620 --> 29:14.060
And notice how this maps perfectly to sphere pop.

29:14.720 --> 29:16.760
Words themselves are collapsed histories.

29:17.440 --> 29:20.560
Why does the sound cat immediately evoke a feline in your mind?

29:20.980 --> 29:31.500
Because millions of English speakers have walked that exact linguistic trajectory through the phonetic snow so many times that the concept collapsed into a stable, single-syllable object.

29:32.200 --> 29:36.400
A word is a reusable module, a pre-compressed trajectory.

29:36.400 --> 29:39.280
And every time we speak, we are walking on the grass.

29:39.760 --> 29:42.160
We are slightly remodeling the substrate of our language.

29:42.680 --> 29:45.700
When a new slang term catches on, it starts as a faint path.

29:46.060 --> 29:48.740
If enough teenagers use it on TikTok, it becomes a dirt path.

29:49.340 --> 29:52.160
Eventually, the dictionary adds it, pouring concrete over it.

29:52.540 --> 29:55.320
Our language is a dynamic, self-modifying substrate.

29:55.840 --> 29:58.340
Now apply this identical logic to software engineering.

29:58.800 --> 30:05.340
If you work in tech, or honestly, if you have ever tried to use a corporate internet, you have intimately felt the pain of substrate deformation.

30:05.340 --> 30:06.500
Oh, absolutely.

30:06.920 --> 30:08.520
Let's make this incredibly practical.

30:09.060 --> 30:10.480
Say you work at a mid-sized company.

30:11.180 --> 30:13.440
The core software platform is 10 years old.

30:14.200 --> 30:20.580
Your boss comes to you and asks to add a seemingly simple new feature, maybe a button that exports a report to PDF.

30:21.140 --> 30:26.340
You look at the code and tell your boss, that will take six months, and it might break the entire billing system.

30:26.860 --> 30:29.780
Your boss assumes you are just a bad, lazy coder.

30:30.360 --> 30:33.120
What is actually happening in the physics of that code base?

30:33.120 --> 30:35.880
What is happening is literal substrate deformation.

30:36.760 --> 30:47.860
Every single past decision, every quick bug fix written at 2 a.m., every rushed future added to that software over the last 10 years permanently deformed the code base's admissibility geometry.

30:48.280 --> 30:49.220
The chessboard is warped.

30:49.720 --> 30:50.520
The clay is sunk.

30:50.640 --> 30:51.080
Exactly.

30:51.540 --> 30:55.820
In software, they call it technical debt, but the paper shows it is not just a clever metaphor.

30:56.080 --> 30:58.300
It is the physical accumulation of path dependence.

30:58.300 --> 31:08.820
The architecture of the code has been so warped by past trajectories that the new feature you want to build the PDF button is now mathematically impossible to reach from the current state without tearing up the floorboards.

31:09.100 --> 31:10.780
You have to refactor the code.

31:11.060 --> 31:15.160
Which, in the language of this paper, is explicit substrate repair.

31:15.160 --> 31:16.940
You have to restore admissibility.

31:17.620 --> 31:26.100
Technical debt is the exact same physical phenomenon as metabolic waste building up in the fluid of your brain or smog chunking a city grid.

31:26.540 --> 31:33.700
It is the dissipation field S from the RSVP model, actively churking the capacity of the system to do anything new.

31:33.920 --> 31:35.100
And it scales up even further.

31:35.500 --> 31:37.620
It scales to human institutions and economics.

31:37.620 --> 31:44.060
Why is it so impossibly hard to change a broken government agency or a terrible health care system?

31:44.500 --> 31:46.580
We usually write it off as historical accident.

31:47.000 --> 31:49.940
Oh, some corrupt politicians passed a bad law 50 years ago.

31:50.060 --> 31:51.160
That's why it's broken today.

31:51.520 --> 31:56.500
But the dynamic substrate theory argues that institutional path dependence is not an accident.

31:57.020 --> 31:58.500
It is a geometric necessity.

31:59.100 --> 32:04.900
The physical infrastructure, the bureaucratic rules, the enforcement mechanisms, these are the transport field.

32:04.900 --> 32:11.000
They are the substrate, and that substrate has been severely deformed by decades of accumulated collective trajectories.

32:11.100 --> 32:17.880
Meaning you can't just elect a new politician, have them pass a new piece of paper called a law, and expect a system to magically fix itself.

32:18.100 --> 32:23.440
No, because passing a law is attempting to make a new decision within the old constraint geometry.

32:23.440 --> 32:29.620
It is like trying to move your knight to a square on the chessboard that has already collapsed into a sinkhole.

32:29.620 --> 32:35.600
Institutional reform, just like software refactoring, requires explicit substrate repair.

32:36.060 --> 32:37.560
You cannot just change the rules.

32:37.700 --> 32:41.600
You have to slowly, painstakingly reshape the geometry itself.

32:41.840 --> 32:45.040
It makes you realize how interconnected everything is.

32:45.580 --> 32:51.480
But I want to bring this grand unified theory back down to the most intimate space possible, the listener's own mind.

32:51.840 --> 32:56.220
I want to rethink what it means to learn, to remember, and to sleep based on this math.

32:56.220 --> 32:59.380
This is perhaps the most paradigm-shifting conclusion of the paper.

32:59.640 --> 33:00.680
Let's start with human memory.

33:01.220 --> 33:05.660
Theorem G.2 in the appendix formally outlines memory without explicit storage.

33:05.880 --> 33:08.520
I found this deeply unsettling, but also beautiful.

33:09.440 --> 33:14.180
We have been trained by the digital age to view our memory as a computer hard drive.

33:14.700 --> 33:19.400
You go to a concert, you experience it, you put it in a mental file folder, and you save it.

33:20.080 --> 33:25.000
Ten years later, you want to remember the concert, so you search your brain, open the folder, and play the file.

33:25.000 --> 33:28.920
The paper says this entire metaphor is completely backwards.

33:29.160 --> 33:30.660
Memory is not a stored state.

33:30.940 --> 33:31.740
There is no file.

33:32.160 --> 33:37.040
Memory is the permanent deformation of your admissibility structure induced by prior trajectories.

33:37.140 --> 33:38.920
Okay, my favorite analogy for this.

33:39.260 --> 33:40.480
Tell me if I'm pushing it too far.

33:41.160 --> 33:42.660
Memory is not a hard drive.

33:43.300 --> 33:44.640
Memory is a dented mattress.

33:44.820 --> 33:45.420
I like that.

33:45.420 --> 33:53.160
When you sleep on a memory foam mattress in the exact same position every single night for five years, it forms a permanent depression in the shape of your body.

33:53.720 --> 33:55.240
The mattress does not remember you.

33:55.540 --> 33:58.000
The mattress doesn't have a file folder with your name on it.

33:58.080 --> 33:59.500
It just permanently took your shape.

34:00.520 --> 34:05.960
The history of your body's physical trajectories permanently deformed the physical substrate of the phone.

34:05.960 --> 34:07.880
It is an excellent physical analog.

34:08.500 --> 34:13.620
You remember how to ride a bicycle not because you access a video file of bike riding in your prefrontal cortex,

34:13.940 --> 34:20.560
but because the electrophluidic substrate of your motocortex, the glial cells, the vascular topology, the synaptic weights,

34:21.080 --> 34:26.500
has been permanently dented into an attractor basin that makes the bike riding trajectory highly admissible.

34:27.040 --> 34:29.600
Your current geometry reflects your past history.

34:29.960 --> 34:30.480
But hold on.

34:30.560 --> 34:31.640
Injecting some friction here.

34:31.640 --> 34:40.840
If I am just a collapsed history of processes, a dented mattress, why do I have such vivid, distinct memories of specific events?

34:41.360 --> 34:43.900
Like my eighth birthday party, I can smell the cake.

34:44.120 --> 34:47.020
That feels exactly like pulling up a file, not rolling into a dent.

34:47.340 --> 34:54.740
It feels like a distinct file to your conscious mind because the attractor basin for that specific sensory convergence is incredibly deep and highly localized.

34:55.420 --> 35:00.840
The sensory triggers the smell of a certain type of frosting act as a very specific coordinate on your manifold.

35:00.840 --> 35:08.580
Once your mental state hits that coordinate, it rapidly falls into the deep basin of that collapsed history, re-experiencing the connected trajectories.

35:09.160 --> 35:14.720
But the storage mechanism is still structural deformation, not a localized data packet.

35:15.100 --> 35:19.600
That completely reframes the first-person experience of learning, the phenomenology of it.

35:19.920 --> 35:21.240
Think about learning to play the guitar.

35:21.420 --> 35:23.680
Why does it feel so impossibly hard on the first day?

35:24.200 --> 35:25.320
Your fingers won't move right.

35:25.380 --> 35:26.220
Your brain hurts.

35:26.220 --> 35:31.500
Because the concept of playing a C chord currently lies entirely outside your admissibility region.

35:32.060 --> 35:36.040
Your brain's constraint geometry literally does not support the action.

35:36.680 --> 35:43.120
It is the equivalent of trying to push a heavy boulder uphill out of a deep basin onto a plateau that doesn't exist yet.

35:43.360 --> 35:47.480
But then you practice every day for a month, and suddenly it clicks.

35:47.960 --> 35:49.160
You don't even look at your hand.

35:49.240 --> 35:50.200
You just play the chord.

35:50.540 --> 35:52.120
It feels effortless.

35:52.640 --> 35:53.080
Fluent.

